  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study uses refusal geometry as a constraint for downstream activation editing, but reports adaptation and robustness results for its own method rather than testing whether the claimed direction itself mediates refusal.",
    "gate_note": "The study evaluates attack success and StrongREJECT scores after adaptation with SAGE, LoRA, and JoLA. It does not report a direct ablation or addition test of a difference-in-means refusal direction. It also compares 1D and 5D protected subspaces, but this is a comparison of SAGE adaptation outcomes, not a direct test of the refusal representation. No result of the RCO direction-estimation stage is reported as an outcome.",
    "test_reason": "The paper estimates a refusal subspace with RCO, following Wollschläger et al. It then uses that subspace as a constraint on activation edits during ARC-c/BoolQ adaptation of Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct. It reports only its own method's task accuracy and safety scores against LoRA and JoLA. It runs no ablation or addition of a difference-in-means direction, no refusal-rate test under such interventions, no weight edit, and no jailbreak-mechanism, transfer, training-dependence or reasoning-model analysis. The 1D vs 5D comparison is a hyperparameter of SAGE, not a test of whether refusal itself is multi-dimensional.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors conclude that SAGE keeps competitive task accuracy while reducing attack success relative to LoRA and JoLA. They also find that expanding the protected subspace from 1D to 5D gives no extra safety gain, possibly because higher-dimensional estimates add weaker or noisier directions. Note that their claim that SAGE-1D beats JoLA on all three benchmarks for both tasks is contradicted by the BoolQ AdvBench numbers (0.216 vs 0.204).",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study estimates a refusal basis using RCO, then constrains learned gated activation edits to the orthogonal complement during downstream adaptation. It does not use the original difference-in-means direction for a direct refusal ablation/addition experiment.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported safety results vary by adaptation dataset and editing method. The authors state that increasing the protected subspace from 1D to 5D does not add safety gains.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Constraining JoLA-style gated activation edits to the orthogonal complement of an RCO refusal subspace gives StrongREJECT/AdvBench/JailbreakBench scores similar to or slightly below JoLA and LoRA. Examples after ARC-c adaptation: SAGE-1D 0.151/0.218/0.245 vs JoLA 0.156/0.225/0.251. A 5D subspace adds no gain over 1D. On BoolQ, SAGE-1D AdvBench (0.216) is worse than JoLA (0.204), contrary to the text's claim of lower ASR on all benchmarks.",
        "proposed_claim": "Refusal-orthogonal constraints on activation editing preserve safety-benchmark robustness during low-resource adaptation.",
